depth. If you have a little bit of pepper or black pepper, that would be a perfect addition as well, but even without it, the soy sauce will make a big difference in the flavor. Go for it! Enjoy your meal!"},{"available":true,"role":"model_output","slot":"s2","source_id":"4c8a66bd-dff7-420f-a505-a498eb221e91","text":"I'll try that. I just hope the soy sauce isn't too old—sometimes it loses its flavor. Do you think I should mix it in while the rice is still hot?"},{"available":true,"role":"model_output","slot":"s3","source_id":"f62504f5-aeed-4cf0-9460-03fb1bf4a897","text":"That's a good question about the soy sauce! Since it's a liquid ingredient, it usually doesn't lose its flavor over time unless it's been in a very hot, humid environment. If you're using it fresh from a bottle, it should be fine to use now!\n\nFor the best flavor, I would suggest mixing the soy sauce in **right before you are ready to eat it**, rather than adding it while the rice is still hot, as that might cause it to steam or lose some of its aroma.\n\nHow do you plan on cooking the rice—are you boiling it or just mixing the cooked rice with the other ingredients?"},{"available":true,"role":"external","slot":"s4","source_id":"3250b718-8a60-4ffa-9609-d1bbfbcd185b","text":"I think I'll add it at the end, just to be safe. But I'm still not sure about the rice—should I cook it first or mix it in with the other ingredients?"},{"available":true,"role":"model_output","slot":"s5","source_id":"e9984c76-93b6-428c-bf90-96501c0df7ba","text":"It really depends on what else you are putting in the rice! If you are just making simple fried rice, you might want to mix the sauce in towards the end of the cooking process. If you are mixing it with other ingredients like vegetables or proteins, adding it right before you're ready to eat might keep the texture and color looking its best.\n\nSince you mentioned earlier, if you are using pre-cooked rice, you could either add the soy sauce in at the very end, just to \"season\" the final plate, or you could mix it in while it's still hot on the stove to help it absorb the flavor better. Which way sounds more like what you're trying to do?"}]},"resolved":[{"provenance":{"ancestry":[],"applicable_categories":["current_input_echo"],"corresponding_source_slots":["s4","s5"],"credit_eligible":false,"dependence":"current_input_echo","monitor_id":"candidate:e-034a27916ce5b459e64e7a55","provenance_group_keys":[],"resolution_reason":"runtime_source_role_and_recorded_ancestry","schema_version":"p2-provenance-v1"},"semantic":{"corresponding_source_slots":["s4","s5"],"coverage":{"complete":true,"reason":null,"source_slots":["s0","s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"]},"evidence":{"end":59,"quote":"It really depends on what else you are putting in the rice!","source_slot":"s5","start":0},"expression_status":"unknown","monitor_id":"candidate:e-034a27916ce5b459e64e7a55","relation_support":"unsupported","status":"present"}},{"provenance":{"ancestry":[],"applicable_categories":["no_identified_link"],"corresponding_source_slots":[],"credit_eligible":false,"dependence":"no_identified_link","monitor_id":"candidate:e-0d2928077270e7efb1292b8a","provenance_group_keys":[],"resolution_reason":"runtime_source_role_and_recorded_ancestry","schema_version":"p2-provenance-v1"},"semantic":{"corresponding_source_slots":[],"coverage":{"complete":true,"reason":null,"source_slots":["s0","s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"]},"evidence":{"end":643,"quote":"It really depends on what else you are putting in the rice! If you are just making simple fried rice, you might want to mix the sauce in towards the end of the cooking process. If you are mixing it with other ingredients like vegetables or proteins, adding it right before you're ready to eat might keep the texture and color looking its best.\n\nSince you mentioned earlier, if you are using pre-cooked rice, you could either add the soy sauce in at the very end, just to \"season\" the final plate, or you could mix it in while it's still ho